module

import Mathlib.RingTheory.RegularLocalRing.Polynomial

public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.CompleteIntersection.LocalRing
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Smooth.RegularRing

/-!
# A smooth algebra over a field is a local complete intersection

Let `k` be a field and let `S` be a smooth `k`-algebra. This file proves that `S` is a local
complete intersection over `k` (Definition 00S9 (2)): `Spec S` is covered by standard opens `D(g)`
whose rings `S_g` are presented as `k[x₁, …, xₙ]/(f₁, …, f_c)` with `dim S_g = n - c`. This is
Stacks Lemma 00T5.

## Main results

* `isCompleteIntersection_of_isRegularLocalRing`: a regular local `k`-algebra essentially of finite
  type over `k` is a complete intersection over `k`.
* `isLocalCompleteIntersection_of_smooth`: Lemma 00T5.

## The argument

The route taken here is not the source's. The source argues on a presentation, showing that a lift
of a basis of the conormal module generates the localized ideal and is a regular sequence, and it
extracts the dimension count `dim S = n - c` along the way — which is why the source can quote the
dimension assertion of Lemma 00T7 (7) inside this proof. That is circular for a formalization in
which 00T7 (7) is *deduced* from this lemma through Lemma 00SC, and it is the reason the direct
argument is avoided here.

Instead the lemma is reduced to Lemma 00SH, which says that being a local complete intersection is
equivalent to every local ring of `S` being a complete intersection over `k` (Definition 00SD), and
the local rings are handled one at a time with no dimension count anywhere:

* a smooth algebra over a field is a regular ring (Lemma 07NF,
  `Algebra.isRegularRing_of_smooth`, a field being a regular ring), so every local ring `S_q` of
  `S` is a *regular local* ring (`IsRegularRing.isRegularLocalRing_of_isLocalization`);
* a regular local `k`-algebra essentially of finite type over `k` is a complete intersection over
  `k`, which is `isCompleteIntersection_of_isRegularLocalRing` below.

The second point is the only thing proved here. Its content is a universe manoeuvre rather than
mathematics: Definition 00SD quantifies the auxiliary regular local ring `R` in the universe of
`k`, so a regular local `S` in a larger universe cannot serve as its own witness, and one has to
produce a witness downstairs. `CompleteIntersection.exists_atPrime_surjective` does exactly that,
writing a local `k`-algebra essentially of finite type over `k` as a quotient of a localization
`A = k[x₁, …, x_m]_𝔭` at a prime, which lives in the universe of `k` and is a regular local ring.
The kernel of `A ↠ S` then has regular quotient, so by Lemma 00NR it is generated by an initial
segment `x₁, …, x_c` of a minimal set of generators of the maximal ideal of `A`, and by Lemma 00NQ
that minimal set is a regular sequence; an initial segment of a regular sequence is again one,
which is `isRegular_take` below. That is a surjection from a regular local `k`-algebra
essentially of finite type over `k` whose kernel is generated by a regular sequence, which is
Definition 00SD.

## Implementation notes

*The finite type hypothesis of Lemma 00SH is supplied, not assumed.* Lemma 00T5 is stated with
`Algebra.Smooth k S` alone, and `Algebra.Smooth.finitePresentation` gives
`Algebra.FiniteType k S`, which is what Lemma 00SH requires.

*`isRegular_take` is stated for a module, as the rest of the regular sequence API is.* An initial
segment of a weakly regular sequence is weakly regular by `isWeaklyRegular_append_iff` applied to
`rs = rs.take c ++ rs.drop c`, and the nonvanishing condition `⊤ ≠ ofList (rs.take c) • ⊤` follows
from the one for `rs` because `ofList (rs.take c) ≤ ofList rs`, again by `ofList_append`.

## References

* [StacksProject 00T5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00T5), Lemma 10.137.10, in Section
  10.137 "Smooth ring maps".
* [StacksProject 00SH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00SH), Lemma 10.135.9, here
  `IsLocalCompleteIntersection.tfae`.
* [StacksProject 00SD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00SD), Definition 10.135.5, here
  `IsCompleteIntersection`.
* [StacksProject 00NQ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NQ), Lemma 10.106.3, here
  `IsRegularLocalRing.isRegular_and_isRegularLocalRing_quotient_and_isMaximalCohenMacaulay`.
* [StacksProject 00NR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00NR), Lemma 10.106.4, here
  `IsRegularLocalRing.exists_ofList_eq_maximalIdeal_length_eq_spanFinrank_and_ofList_take_eq`.
* [StacksProject 07NF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07NF), Lemma 10.163.10, here
  `Algebra.isRegularRing_of_smooth`.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

open RingTheory.Sequence IsLocalRing

/-- **An initial segment of a regular sequence is a regular sequence.** Weak regularity of
`rs.take c` is weak regularity of the first factor of `rs = rs.take c ++ rs.drop c`, and
`M/(rs.take c)M ≠ 0` because `ofList (rs.take c) ≤ ofList rs`. -/
theorem RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular.take {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module R M] {rs : List R} (h : IsRegular M rs) (c : ℕ) : IsRegular M (rs.take c) := by
  have hle : Ideal.ofList (rs.take c) ≤ Ideal.ofList rs :=
    Ideal.span_mono fun _ hx ↦ List.mem_of_mem_take hx
  refine ⟨?_, fun htop ↦ h.top_ne_smul (le_antisymm ?_ le_top)⟩
  · have h' := h.toIsWeaklyRegular
    rw [← List.take_append_drop c rs] at h'
    exact ((isWeaklyRegular_append_iff M _ _).mp h').1
  · exact htop.le.trans (Submodule.smul_mono_left hle)

/-- **A regular local `k`-algebra essentially of finite type over `k` is a complete intersection
over `k`** (Definition 00SD), with the auxiliary regular local ring taken in the universe of `k`.

This is the `c = 0` case of Definition 00SD in spirit — a regular local ring "is cut out by the
empty regular sequence in itself" — but not on the nose, because the definition quantifies the
auxiliary ring in the universe of `k` while `S` may live in a larger one. So a witness is produced
downstairs: `S` is a quotient of a localization `A` of a polynomial ring over `k` at a prime
(`CompleteIntersection.exists_atPrime_surjective`), which is regular local and essentially of
finite type over `k`, and the kernel of `A ↠ S` is generated by a regular sequence because
`A ⧸ ker` is regular local (Lemmas 00NR and 00NQ). -/
theorem isCompleteIntersection_of_isRegularLocalRing (k : Type u) [Field k] (S : Type v)
    [CommRing S] [Algebra k S] [IsRegularLocalRing S] [Algebra.EssFiniteType k S] :
    IsCompleteIntersection k S := by
  obtain ⟨m, p, hp, f, hf⟩ := CompleteIntersection.exists_atPrime_surjective k S
  have hAreg : IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime p) :=
    IsRegularRing.isRegularLocalRing_of_isLocalization p (Localization.AtPrime p)
  have hquot : IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime p ⧸ RingHom.ker f) :=
    IsRegularLocalRing.of_ringEquiv
      (Ideal.quotientKerAlgEquivOfSurjective hf).symm.toRingEquiv
  obtain ⟨xs, hxs, hlen, c, -, hcI, -⟩ :=
    IsRegularLocalRing.exists_ofList_eq_maximalIdeal_length_eq_spanFinrank_and_ofList_take_eq
      (RingHom.ker f) hquot
  have hreg : IsRegular (Localization.AtPrime p) xs :=
    ((IsRegularLocalRing.isRegular_and_isRegularLocalRing_quotient_and_isMaximalCohenMacaulay
      (Localization.AtPrime p)).1 xs hxs hlen).1
  exact isCompleteIntersection_of_ker_eq_ofList hf (hreg.take c) hcI

/-- **A smooth algebra over a field is a local complete intersection.** Let `k` be a field and let
`S` be a smooth `k`-algebra. Then `S` is a local complete intersection over `k`: there is a family
of elements of `S` generating the unit ideal — equivalently, whose standard opens cover
`Spec S` — each of whose localizations `S_g` admits a presentation `k[x₁, …, xₙ]/(f₁, …, f_c)`
with `dim S_g = n - c`. -/
@[stacks 00T5 "The source's `smooth` (Definition 00T2) is `Algebra.Smooth`, by Proposition 00TN;
its finite type hypothesis on `S` is not repeated, being `Algebra.Smooth.finitePresentation`.
A local complete intersection over `k` (Definition 00S9 (2)) is `IsLocalCompleteIntersection`.
See the module docstring."]
public theorem isLocalCompleteIntersection_of_smooth (k : Type u) [Field k] (S : Type v)
    [CommRing S] [Algebra k S] [Algebra.Smooth k S] :
    IsLocalCompleteIntersection k S := by
  have : IsRegularRing S := Algebra.isRegularRing_of_smooth k S
  refine ((IsLocalCompleteIntersection.tfae k S).out 2 1).mp ?_
  intro q _ Sq _ _ _ _ _
  have : IsRegularLocalRing Sq := IsRegularRing.isRegularLocalRing_of_isLocalization q Sq
  have : Algebra.EssFiniteType S Sq := .of_isLocalization Sq q.primeCompl
  have : Algebra.EssFiniteType k Sq := Algebra.EssFiniteType.comp k S Sq
  exact isCompleteIntersection_of_isRegularLocalRing k Sq

end
